you're watching sky news at four o'clock  and we start this hour with breaking  news the release of a damning report  into british gymnastics and the abuse of  young children the report exposes a  brutal culture inside the sport with  young gymnasts starved and forced to go  through humiliating punishments  the review examined hundreds of  complaints between 2008 and 2020 to  uncover the regime imposed by some  coaches and it says that countless young  people were shamed and permanently  damaged both physically and  psychologically  well the review by mary white qc covered  a 12-year period during which british  gymnastics was estimated to have  received 3500 complaints the majority of  its members during that period were  children under the age of 12.  the white review published today  examined 400 complaints and said an  unacceptable culture within british  gymnastics left countless people  humiliated shamed and damaged by their  time in the sport  67 percent of those spoke of emotional  abuse 49 of physical abuse and nine  percent of sexual abuse  more than a quarter of submissions  reported excessive weight management as  a particular form of abusive behavior  well our sports correspondent tom  parmenter has more on this breaking news  so tom as we say a really damning  verdict on some of the experiences that  these young gymnasts  had during these years  it is a quite breathtaking report  actually having read it over the last  few hours and only now can we report the  full details of what the right white  review lays out and it is a catalogue of  abuse both emotional and physical abuse  within british gymnastics that really  just  astonishes anyone i think who reads it  it shows quite graphically stories from  gyms from around the united kingdom of  children being abused as part of their  sports of them being punished for being  late punishments which would include  being made to hang from the rings inside  a gymnasium or one youngster who was  forced to balance on a beam for two  hours as part of a punishment for being  late the culture within british  gymnastics that is laid bare has been  talked about for some time but to have  it all collated into one place is a  damning indictment of those coaches  involved not every coach within  gymnastics of course but those coaches  that have been involved in this abuse uh  really  it is  something that beggars belief just what  children have been put through in the  name of sports and british gymnastics  the organization the governing body who  are supposed to have oversight on all  this are singled out for  damning criticism that says that there  was just a culture of silence a culture  of just accepting that these things were  the norm and a culture in which  complaints if they were raised and many  didn't even feel able to raise them such  was the relationship between some  coaches and athletes that if those  complaints did make it any further there  wasn't a culture of investigating those  things properly  the numbers involved are some 400 people  who have given evidence to the white  review but in reality there are many  many other youngsters predominantly  girls who have gone through the  gymnastic system over the last couple of  decades who report similar uh  accusations of bullying of body shaming  of being made to feel just worthless as  part of their sport of being  punished as we say but also starving  themselves in the name of sport such was  the culture around body image and weight  that the scales of tyranny was one of  the phrases that was used in the way  that coaches would do daily weigh-ins  for gymnasts and publicly  name the figures that they were reaching  in their body weights and the corrosive  effect of all that on young people and  their aspirations uh is just  extraordinary we've been speaking to  many gymnasts who have been through  horrific experiences in their early  lives that have in some cases destroyed  childhoods and have long lasting  legacies we've also spoken to  jennifer jennifer mcilveen who  is from a body called gymnast for change  who have campaigned to raise the profile  of this abuse going on within british  gymnastics and she told us that what  we've seen outlined today is a  vindication of all those athletes who  have come forward and shared their  experiences and also it's the moment for  change  it's absolutely child abuse i've seen  comments online people saying oh they're  just not tough enough they don't  understand what it takes to be an elite  athlete it's absolutely uncalled for as  you can see from the testimonials in  this report we're talking about serious  physical in 40 of the cases in the white  review physical abuse emotional  psychological abuse that leads to  serious trauma eating disorders and ptsd  and in some cases sexual abuse this is  not tough coaching this is an epidemic  of abuse that we're talking about  so this damning report what's the  response been what are the sporting  bodies saying about it  well at the heart of this particular  shaming report within sport a young  people because the vast majority of  those athletes involved are children  and the governing bodies have a lot of  very difficult questions to deal with  and we've been putting some of those to  uk sport who have oversight of this but  also more pointedly to british  gymnastics the governing body who are  singled out as an insular organization  who have just failed to appreciate the  scale and culture that has been going on  within their sport and the report  outlines a disconnect between the  governing body and those within the  community of gymnastics in the uk we  spoke earlier to the new chief executive  of british british gymnastics sarah  powell who told us that she is truly  sorry for what  many many athletes have had to  experience at the hands of abusive  coaches over the years and that she is  determined to ensure that the  organization puts things right so that  children their families can have faith  and confidence that when they go to a  british gymnastics gym or event that  they are in a safe environment  my overall riding response is one of  emotion and  it was not  good reading and it was quite harrowing  and emotional  sports been so important to me  throughout my life and to see that the  experiences of those gymnasts has not  been a positive one  is heart-wrenching um and we clearly  haven't put the welfare of gymnasts at  the heart of what we do at british  gymnastics  so the governing body is certainly in no  doubt as to the scale of the challenge  that they now face to rebuild trust with  athletes and to try to put right some of  the wrongs of the past to ensure that  the horrific experiences that so many  particularly young girls have gone  through through gymnastics  really is never allowed to be repeated  but significantly uh we put to the uh  chief executive that  not many coaches have actually been  sanctioned and some indeed are still  working within the sport and she says  that that is a situation that cannot  allow be allowed to continue they have  to be evidence-based in the  investigations and decisions that they  take but they acknowledge that  punishments should follow and  potentially bans or significant fines  for the behavior of the past but she  said this is about creating a better  path for the future to ensure that  people do have confidence in the way  that british gymnastics is operating and  that those good coaches out there have  the vindication that they have a  governing body that is operating in the  right way that is fit for a modern  society because quite simply what we've  uncovered or seen uncovered in this  report is that british gymnastics was  not fit for purpose and that abusive  coaches were getting away with the  physical and emotional abuse of  youngsters to the detriment of so many  lives and today in many ways is a  testament to the way that so many of  them have decided to come forward and  speak about their experiences often  deeply personal traumatic episodes that  have had long lasting effects on  gymnasts even when they've left the  sport of gymnastics and today is that  moment of change they hope for their  sport a sport that of course so many  people love and participate in but that  context and framework around it has  failed for so for too long and now the  challenge for the sporting authorities  is that that needs to change and one  footnote to that is that the report  calls for a sport ombudsman that would  be a regulator a independent  body that would allow people to approach  a body that they trust with complaints  and for those complaints to be looked at  independently and fairly uh that will of  course need a response from the uk  government but stinging criticism today  for the sport of gymnastics and more  widely i think across the world of sport  a big wake-up call to the  culture that has been allowed to fester  within gymnastics and what that means  around safeguarding the protection of  young athletes particularly going  forward
 

